The standard approach in .NET is to **wrap file system calls behind an interface** and inject it:

```csharp
public interface IFileSystem
{
    string ReadAllText(string path);
    void WriteAllText(string path, string contents);
    bool DirectoryExists(string path);
}
```

Then inject `IFileSystem` into your classes. In tests, mock it (e.g., with `NSubstitute` or `Moq`).

**Alternatives:**

1. **`System.IO.Abstractions`** — a popular NuGet package that provides `IFileSystem` out of the box, plus an in-memory implementation (`MockFileSystem`) for tests.
2. **`Microsoft.Extensions.FileProviders`** — useful for read-only scenarios.

**Recommendation:** Use [`System.IO.Abstractions`](https://www.nuget.org/packages/System.IO.Abstractions). It covers the full `System.IO` surface, requires minimal refactoring (replace `File.X` with `_fileSystem.File.X`), and ships a test helper package:

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

```csharp
// Production
public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem) { ... }

// Test
var fs = new MockFileSystem();
fs.AddFile("/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{}"));
var svc = new MyService(fs);
```

This keeps your code unit-testable without touching the real disk.
